      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224420",
      ":question/problem": "Previous studies of a particular city district show that buses arrive late, on average, $15$ times per day. The transport agency retrained its drivers and renewed its fleet to improve the bus service. The agency then decided to conduct a statistical hypothesis test at the $5%$ significance level to see whether there had been a reduction in the number of buses arriving late.\n\nThe agency randomly selected a day in a particular month and found $12$ buses arrived late that day.\n\nLet $\u03bb$ be the average number of late buses each day, and let $X$ equal the number of late buses on a randomly selected day. By assuming that the late buses are independent, fill in the missing information below:\n\nThe null and alternative hypotheses are:\n\n$H_{0}$: {{field-1}}\n\n$H_{1}$: {{field-2}}\n\nThe distribution of $X$ under the null hypothesis is $X\u223c$ {{field-3}}.\n\nA suitable test statistic is $P($ {{field-4}} $)$."
    },
