      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224586",
      ":question/problem": "A baker collected information over several months of the last few years and determined that her store sold, on average, $24$ cakes per week. The baker invests in advertising and renews the image of her store. She decided then to conduct a statistical hypothesis test at the $5%$ significance level to see whether there had been an increase in the number of cakes sold weekly.\n\nThe baker randomly selected a week in a particular month and found that $28$ cakes were sold that week.\n\nLet $\u03bb$ be the average number of cakes sold each week, and $X$ be the number of cakes sold on a randomly selected week. By assuming that the sales are independent, fill in the missing information below:\n\nThe null and alternative hypotheses are:\n\n$H_{0}$: {{field-1}}\n\n$H_{1}$: {{field-2}}\n\nThe distribution of $X$ under the null hypothesis is $X\u223c$ {{field-3}}.\n\nA suitable test statistic is $P($ {{field-4}} $)$."
    }
